hat stabilize B-DNA, while it can be fueled by adding Z-DNA-promoting high-saline buffer. When MgCl(2) was used as external stimulus, 70% of the motors rotated, while 76% of the stators/controls exhibited no rotation. Such a motor system could be successfully applied to perform multiple actions aimed for our benefit. Moreover, for the first time we have directly observed the B-Z conformational transition of DNA in real-time, which shed light on the fundamental understanding of DNA conformations.\n\n## Supplementary resources (2)\n\nja310454k si 002\n\nData\n\nMarch 2013\n\nArivazhagan Rajendran · Masayuki Endo · Kumi Hidaka · Hiroshi Sugiyama\n\nja310454k si 001\n\nData\n\nMarch 2013\n\nArivazhagan Rajendran · Masayuki Endo · Kumi Hidaka · Hiroshi Sugiyama\n\n## Citations (73)\n\n## References (47)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn experimental study of the putative mechanism of a synthetic autonomous rotary DNA nanomotor (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5383820/)\nciteturn55search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Volume: 135 , First page: 1117 –Last page: 1123 . ([ext-link-type: uri] doi:10.1021/ja310454k)Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 23311576 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] [assigning-authority: pmc] 10.1021/ja310454k\n\n16 [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Yan H , [name-style: western] Zhang X , [name-style: western] Shen Z , [name-style: western] Seeman NC\n\nYear: 2002 A robust DNA mechanical device controlled by hybridization topology . Nature Volume: 415 , First page: 62 –Last page: 65 . ([ext-link-type: uri] doi:10.1038/415062a)Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 11780115 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] [assigning-authority: pmc] 10.1038/415062a\n\n17 [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Rajendran A , [name-style: western] Endo M , [name-style: western] Hidaka K , [name-style: western] Sugiyama H\n\nYear: 2013 Direct and real-time observation of rotary movement of a DNA nanomechanical device . J. Am. Chem. Soc. Volume: 135 , First page: 1117 –Last page: 1123 . ([ext-link-type: uri] doi:10.1021/ja310454k)Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 23311576 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] [assigning-authority: pmc] 10.1021/ja310454k\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirect and real-time observation of rotary movement of a DNA nanomechanical device (https://documentsdelivered.com/source/007/639/007639590.php)\nciteturn55search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI: 10.1021/ja310454k\n\n# Direct and real-time observation of rotary movement of a DNA nanomechanical device\n\n##### Rajendran, A.; Endo, M.; Hidaka, K.; Sugiyama, H.\n\n* * *\n\n#### Journal of the American Chemical Society 135(3): 1117-1123\n\nISSN/ISBN: 1520-5126\n\n* * *\n\n#### 2013\n\n* * *\n\nPMID: 23311576\nDOI: 10.1021/ja310454k\nDocument: 007639590\n\nAnalogous to the biologically abundant protein-based linear molecular machines that translocate along their target surface, we have recently constructed the DNA-based synthetic molecular motors that effect linear movement or navigate a network of tracks on a DNA origami substrate. However, a DNA-based molecular machine with rotary function, analogous to rotary proteins, is still unexplored. Here, we report the construction of a rotary motor based on the B-Z conformational transition of DNA and the direct and real-time observation of its function within a frame-shaped DNA origami. The motor can be switched off by introducing conditions that stabilize B-DNA, while it can be fueled by adding Z-DNA-promoting high-saline buffer. When MgCl(2) was used as external stimulus, 70% of the motors rotated, while 76% of the stators/controls exhibited no rotation. Such a motor system could be successfully applied to perform multiple actions aimed for our benefit. Moreover, for the first time we have directly observed the B-Z conformational transition of DNA in real-time, which shed light on the fundamental understanding of DNA conformations.\n\nImage--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNature-Inspired Design of Smart Biomaterials Using the Chemical Biology of Nucleic Acids | Bulletin of the Chemical Society of Japan | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/bcsj/article/89/8/843/7329788)\nciteturn55search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; PubMed ... 10.1021/ja310454k\n\n,\n\nY.\n\nSuzuki\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nAngew. Chem., Int. Ed.\n\n2012\n\n,\n\n51\n\n,\n\n10518\n\n.\n\n10.1002/anie.201205247\n\nCrossref\n\nPubMed\n\n \n\nb)\n\nY.\n\nTakeuchi\n\n,\n\nM.\n\nEndo\n\n,\n\nY.\n\nSuzuki\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nG.\n\nDurand\n\n,\n\nE.\n\nDausse\n\n,\n\nJ.-J.\n\nToulmé\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nBiomater. Sci.\n\n2016\n\n,\n\n4\n\n,\n\n130\n\n.\n\n10.1039/C5BM00274E\n\nCrossref\n\nPubMed\n\nPubMed\n\n \n\n58\n\na)\n\nM.\n\nEndo\n\n,\n\nM.\n\nInoue\n\n,\n\nY.\n\nSuzuki\n\n,\n\nC.\n\nMasui\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nMorinaga\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nChem.—Eur. J.\n\n2013\n\n,\n\n19\n\n,\n\n16887\n\n.\n\n10.1002/chem.201303830\n\nCrossref\n\nPubMed\n\nPubMed\n\n \n\nb)\n\nA.\n\nRajendran\n\n,\n\nM.\n\nEndo\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nJ. Am. Chem. Soc.\n\n2013\n\n,\n\n135\n\n,\n\n1117\n\n.\n\n10.1021/ja310454k\n\nCrossref\n\nPubMed\n\nPubMed\n\n \n\n59\n\na)\n\nY.\n\nSannohe\n\n,\n\nM.\n\nEndo\n\n,\n\nY.\n\nKatsuda\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nJ. Am. Chem. Soc.\n\n2010\n\n,\n\n132\n\n,\n\n16311\n\n.\n\n10.1021/ja1058907\n\nCrossref\n\nPubMed\n\nPubMed\n\n \n\nb)\n\nY.\n\nYang\n\n,\n\nM.\n\nEndo\n\n,\n\nY.\n\nSuzuki\n\n,\n\nK.\n\nHidaka\n\n,\n\nH.\n\nSugiyama\n\n,\n\nChem. Commun.\n\n2014\n\n,\n\n50\n\n,\n\n4211\n\n.\n\n10.1039/c4cc00489b\n\nCrossref\n\n------------------------------------------